Well, after a five year or so hiatus, and motivated by the work ais523
and
dwangoAC are performing on finding the fastest possible solution to
the
game, I decided to refresh my knowledge of the current state of
affairs. Lo
and behold, another character ascended on nethack.alt.org (so
recordings of
the game and the full dumplog are available if you look there), making
for
two in total.

This is her story.

----

Essie awoke with a start, very much alive and healthy in the physical
sense. The mental trauma of imagining being slain in a dark cavern by
a
gnome of some noble persuasion, however, would linger a while longer.

It was the latest in a series of recurring nightmares which had
occurred
for the past several nights. The particulars varied considerably, but
they
each had one major element in common: they ended with Essie's horrible
death in many painful ways, from being set on fire to being beaten to
death
by an invisible assailant to being fried to a crisp by a vengeful
deity.
Needless to say, repeatedly envisioning one's own demise was not
particularly conducive to leading a sound lifestyle.

The door to Essie's room creaked open to reveal an acolyte standing at
the
threshold. He stared at her for a moment, frowning; she returned the
stare
coldly.

"Oh, you're awake already?" said the acolyte. "Another nightmare?"

"Obviously," snapped Essie. "Do I look the type to suddenly wake up
for no
good reason?"

A tense moment passed.

"In any case," the acolyte continued, "the Arch Priest sent me to
collect
you. He apparently has a matter of urgency to discuss with you."

"Let me guess," said Essie. "He's prepared to toss me out of the
temple
once and for all, finally. I'm surprised it's taken this long--"

"Er," said the acolyte. "Perhaps in a manner of speaking that's true -
he
did say you might not be expected to return - but he refused to
explain
anything else to anyone who wasn't you."

Essie stared at the acolyte for a moment. That was unusual behaviour
on the
part of the temple's illustrious leader - he normally would ramble at
length to anyone who was even remotely interested in listening to his
tirades.

"Come on, then," continued the acolyte. "It's best not to keep His
Holiness
waiting."

----

"I have heard from the other acolytes," said the Arch Priest, "that
you
have been having a series of, ah, nightmares as late. Is this true?"

Essie regarded the stony features of her ostensible spiritual leader
curiously, but his face betrayed nothing of where this conversation
was
going.

She decided to play it safe for the moment. "Yes," she said.

The Arch Priest sighed. "Tell me about them."

"Certainly," said Essie deferentially, "but I warn you that what you
hear
may not be entirely pleasant."

"I have witnessed far more horrors during my ample time in this world
than
a simple acolyte such as yourself can imagine," said the Arch Priest.
"I am
more than prepared to weather whatever tales you can offer me."

Essie frowned again. Gathering her thoughts, she launched into her
tale -
of finding herself in an unfamiliar series of catacombs, of feeling an
urgent need to explore and progress further into the mess, and of
meeting
her doom in a variety of horrible fashions.

The Arch Priest did not find it pleasant.

"I see," he said heavily, and added, under his breath, "Of all the
acolytes, why did it have to be you?"

"Sorry?" said Essie.

"Never mind," said the Arch Priest.

"Is there a reason you wanted to know more about these?" asked Essie.
"Let
me guess - there is some deeper meaning to all of this that you are
about to
reveal to me in great detail?"

The Arch Priest sighed again. "Your insight, as usual, is singular,
though
I maintain that it would be much better served without the sarcasm.

"Yes, dear acolyte. It is a sign - a sign of what is to come - a sign
that
you have been chosen to carry out a quest of singular importance."

Essie shuddered, not liking where this conversation was going. "Go
on," she
said, reaching for the glass of water she'd set out on the desk.

"You have been tasked with locating the Amulet of Yendor--"

Essie sprayed the sip of water she'd just taken back onto the desk.
"The
Amulet--? Is that not just a legend?"

"No, my dear," said the Arch Priest. "Though it may have been lost to
the
ages, it is very real, very powerful, and very important."

"How?"

"None alive can say."

"Hmph," said Essie. "If nobody knows why it's so important--"

"Loki has decreed that it shall be retrieved, and so it shall be
done."

A thunderclap punctuated this statement. Given that it otherwise
appeared
to be a bright, sunny day, this could not have been a coincidence, and
thus
Essie bit back the snide retort she had been about to make.

The Arch Priest apparently took her silence as a cue. "Listen closely,
then, for I have much to tell you of before your journey begins..."

----

It was hours before the Arch Priest finally bade Essie leave to rest
to
prepare for her journey, though the knowledge that he had shared with
her
were considerably more interesting than one would have been led to
believe
from his past monologues. She left with a set of instructions to pass
along
to her fellow acolytes and a strict prohibition on informing anyone
else as
to the true purpose of what she was up to.

It was fortunate that the instructions were written and bore the seal
of
the Arch Priest, or none of the acolytes would have been likely to
take the
matter with any seriousness at all. Thus, instead of making more
comments
on Essie's theorised mental instability, they instead rallied around a
comment made by the temple's blacksmith, upon receipt of the Arch
Priest's
orders, that she was reduced to doing the Arch Priest's grunt work.

Thus, as the temple busily equipped Essie for her upcoming journey,
she
quickly received the appelation "Essie Grunt", which was quickly
shortened
to "S. Grunt".

----

The following morning, wearing her usual robe which had been blessed
specially by the Arch Priest, and toting a similarly-blessed mace,
with a
small wooden shield that had been hewn to match the design, Essie
debarked
on her journey. Packed in her knapsack was just enough food to get her
to
the entrance of the ominously-named Mazes of Menace, two dusty old
spellbooks she had given a casual read-over but not understood
particularly
well, four vials of holy water, and a small bell; at her side was one
of
the temple's pet kittens, who the Arch Priest had apparently decreed
at the
last moment was to accompany her.

The journey to the dungeon was uneventful, and she finished the last
of her
rations, leaving a clove of garlic and a sprig of wolfsbane as the
leftovers, just before venturing down the set of stairs to take on her
destiny.

As she reached the end of the staircase and glanced around the large
room
she found herself in - looking exactly as though it had been lifted
from
one of her nightmares - she could have sworn that she heard a faint
voice
for a moment:

"Hello SGrunt, welcome to NetHack!  You are a chaotic elven
Priestess."

----

Essie's immediate plan of action was based around what the Arch Priest
had
informed her of the structure of the upper levels of the area known at
the
Dungeons of Doom, itself comprised of the upper levels of the Mazes of
Menace proper. According to the Arch Priest, there was firstly
rumoured to
be a series of mines run by gnomes, in the middle of which was a small
settlement, and at the bottom of which was supposedly a stone that,
when
carried, imbued good fortune upon its bearer. Elsewhere in the
Dungeons,
there was supposedly a mysterious series of arrangement of boulders
and
pits, beyond which was a great treasure, though opinions were divided
as to
whether said treasure was an amulet capable of reflecting many magical
attacks or a bag capable of storing innumerable items while reducing
the
weight thereof.

The plan was therefore to first seek out this so-called Minetown, to
serve
as a possible future base of operations, and then to go seek out the
'great
treasure' at the far end of the so-called Sokoban puzzles. This
treasure,
she felt, would ensure she was adequately prepared to venture to the
end of
the mines and retrieve the luckstone.

Her immediate objective, then, was to find the entrance to the mines,
the
entrance of which was supposedly one to three levels below her point
of
entry.

Setting out through one of the three passages in the initial room, she
eventually arrived in another room on the first floor, stepping on a
newt
in the process. There were several items strewn about the room, among
them
another spellbook (which she flipped through a couple of times and
subsequently stowed in her backpack) and a whistle, producing a
familiar
high whistling when she experimentally blew it.

Of greater interest, however, was the innocuous lamp sitting in the
corner.
She knew well the rumours that such flasks often contained djinni,
who,
should they feel particularly benevolent, would grant those who
released
them a single wish. What were the chances that this would be one of
those
vessels?

Sensing no signs of a divine curse from the lamp, she carefully poured
one
of her vials of holy water over it and muttered the incantation to
grant it
Loki's blessings, and proceeded to rub the water over the lamp.

To her great surprise and delight, as she rubbed the lamp, a large
could of
smoke emerged, in which was visible the figure of a djinni.

"I am in your debt," said the djinni. "I will grant one wish!"

Essie stared at the djinni. "I don't suppose I can wish that it was
someone
else carrying out this quest instead of me, can I?"

The djinni frowned at her. "You may wish for an object," he said.

Essie thought for a moment, taking the evasive reply as a sign that
she was
committed to this quest whether she wanted to be or not. The most
important
thing, then, was survival, and what better way to survive than the
finest
armour in existence?

Legends had spoken of suits of armour crafted from the scales of
dragons,
which granted their wearer many magical forms of protection. The most
sought after, it had been said, were those of the silver dragon -
whose
mirror-like sheen reflected attacks in a fashion said to be eerily
similar
to that of one of the theorised 'great treasures' of Sokoban - and
those of
the gray dragon - whose scaled were said to absorb many other forms of
magic. Surprisingly, even these mystical armours could be further
enchanted
by conventional magical means.

The Arch Priest had further informed her that in most cases, it was
best
for items to receive the blessings of Loki (or possibly another
deity), and
that many items were vulnerable to fire, rust, or other forms of
corrosion
but could be protected by various means sometimes referred to as
"fixing"
the item, and further by applying a layer of grease to the item.

"For what do you wish?" prompted the djinni.

With careful enunciation, Essie recited,

"blessed fixed greased +3 silver dragon scale mail"

In a puff of smoke, the djinni disappeared, and in Essie's
outstretched
arms appeared a silvery scaled suit of armour, shiny only insofar as
the
layer of grease that had been applied over it allowed it to be, and
clearly
carrying the blessings of some deity.

It was, indeed, a wish come true. She quickly disrobed and donned the
suit
of armour, verifying with trained magical intuition that it was
enchanted
as she had requested, and placed the robe overtop of the armour.

With a newfound sense of optimism, she continued to roam through the
passageways on the first level of the dungeon.

----

It was not long before Essie stumbled across a bright floral-patterend
shirt. As out of place as such an item seemed in the dungeon (were
there
perhaps tourists who sought such a strange locale out as an
opportunity for
sightseeing?), she recognised that even such a strange piece of
clothing
provided the opportunity for magical enchantment, and further that it
was
small enough that it would fit under her newfound silver dragon scale
mail.

As she did not sense the telltale signs of a curse, she quickly
removed her
garmaments and donned the shirt. To her surprise, she detected that
the
shirt already carried a residual minor magical enchantment -
apparently she
wasn't the only such person to recognise the value of the shirt.

----

Eventually, Essie stumbled across a downward staircase; ironically, it
was
in the room just on the other side of the last of the three passages
in the
entry hall. With minor trepidation largely assuaged by her good
fortune,
she descended the stairs only to find herself in another room not too
dissimilar to the one she had just left, though she knew that she was
a
floor down from where she had begun.

Some additional exploration yielded a downwards staircase in the third
room
she entered, but it was different subtly from the previous two; it had
the
look of predating most of the rest of the dungeon and look more
roughly
hewn, though that in itself would not indicate very much.

She descended this second set of stairs, and quickly realised that the
first part of her planof attack was now properly underway - the stairs
gave
way to a dark area, the only nearby walls of which were natural rock,
and
she made an educated guess that she had found the fabled Gnomish
Mines. Far
off in the darkness she spied a gnome of some measure of nobility
judging
by the manner in which he carried himself, which only served to
confirm
that guess.

She returned upstairs quickly to collect the temple's pet kitten, then
descended into the darkness.

----

Essie very quickly made two observations about the gnomes from the
gnome
lord she first encountered: they were slow, and they were easy to
kill. As
weak as she was right then, it was very easy for her to smash the
gnome
lord over the head with her mace and then fall back a few steps,
forcing
him to cover the distance between the two before he could
counterattack; it
only took two or three solid hits (interspersed with as many misses)
before
the last blow reduced the noble gnome to a bloody paste.

"This," Essie muttered to herself, "is revenge for the last time you
killed
me."

With that messy introduction to the mines, Essie began her search for
the
passageway to the next level.

It wasn't long before Essie suddenly found herself in unfamiliar
surroundings mid-step. She paused for a moment - had she just been
teleported?

She heard the mewing of the kitten off in the distance, considerably
further away from where she had been a moment ago, and deduced that
that
was the most likely possibility. Fearing for the kitten, she set back
off
in that direction.

Along the way she encountered a short, hairy-footed creature she had
sometimes heard called hobbits. The hobbit set upon her with a dagger
of
Elvish origin in hand, but she managed to slay him with little
difficulty.

Holding the dagger reminded her of stories she had heard of gifts
granted
to Loki's faithful in the past - they would occasionally receive
weapons of
great power, ranging from simple daggers that would warn of the
presence of
orcs to a broadsword easily capable of slaying those same orcs to a
blade
that would drain the life of those it was used against. Of course, if
one
already had two of those three, it would make obtaining the last
significantly easier.

The dagger she held was very similar in craft to those falling into
the
first category, and the idea behind the last was the most appealing to
her;
thus, upon the dagger she held, she inscribed the name she had heard
its
brethren called before - Sting.

As she finished doing so, to her minor surprise, she sensed a small
surge
of magic from the dagger, sensing that it had magically taken on the
name
and properties of its brethren. Perhaps this was how the other heroes
of
legend had obtained the blade?

In any case, that was one of the two treasures she needed, being an
elf, to
ensure - should Loki see fit to do so - that any divine weapon she
received
in the future would be the bloodthirsty blade she so coveted.

----

Presently, Essie found the area she had disappeared from a few moments
ago,
and heard the sound of the temple's kitten from just beyond. Between
the
two, however, she sensed the teleportation trap that had catapulted
her
elsewhere, and the passage around it was currently too narrow to
maneuver
around - if this was a mine, however, a digging tool such as a pick-
axe
should not be difficult to find.

"Wait here," she whispered to the kitten. "I'll come back to let you
by."

The kitten mewed softly, and Essie turned to continue her search.

----

As Essie ventured through the level of the mines, she eventually
procured a
hard pair of shoes and a helmet from a dwarf, who was not,
unfortunately,
carrying the sought-after digging implement; it was, however, a
welcome
relief from her previous bare-footed expeditioning.

Though it took some time, and the vanquishing of several other
creatures,
she did eventually find the passage to the next lower floor of the
mines,
seemingly clear on the opposite side of the caverns from where she had
begun. The next level was brightly lit, in stark opposition to the
darkness
she had just arrived from; as ever, gnomes were wandering about,
though one
sporting a primitive-looking crown caught her attention immediately.

She quickly deduced that he was a much better fighter than some of his
kin,
but - barring a brief retreat to the previous floor to recover from a
wound
she had received - he died just the same as the rest of his kin.

Shortly after fighting the rest of the small group off, she stepped on
another teleportation trap - but she did not land far from where she
stared; indeed, by taking a couple of steps in what she thought was
north,
she could see the trap again.

As she did so, she spotted a single, gigantic eyeball floating slowly
towards her. She remembered the tales she had heard of such creatures
-
their gazes could paralyze the unwary, leaving them for dead at the
hands
of whatever other monsters were nearby.

Essie glanced down at her magical armour and smiled slightly. Then she
went
and bashed the eye with her mace.

It whirled around to face her - but the piercing gaze was reflected
from
the magical scales of her armour. She swung her mace again and pulpled
the
eye against the stone floor.

Just behind the pulpy remains of the eye was the passage to the next
area.

----

Essie descended the stairs into a room reminiscent of those she had
left
three levels above in the Dungeons of Doom. As though this was not
enough
of an omen, travelling down a nearby passage led to a large open
cavern
with smoothed floors - significantly more civilised than the areas she
had
just departed.

She surmised - correctly, as it turned out - that she had arrived in
Minetown.

Wandering around the nearest building, she beheld, to her surprise, a
stone
giant. Though she knew, courtesy of the Arch Priest, that they resided
in
the Mazes of Menace, she had been led to believe that they were much
deeper
denizens therein - perhaps this one had been drawn here by another
fallen
adventurer?

As though to answer her question, on either end of the northerly
street
came into view a warg and a blob of acid. Except possibly for the
blob,
neither of these creatures would have had any reason to be up this far
in
the Mazes save for the involvement of another adventurer.

Realising the danger the blob posed to her equipment, she confronted
the
warg and slew it, though not without taking several painful bites.
Worse,
another warg saw the opportunity to charge down the street towards
her.

Realising she might not survive such a confrontation, she quickly
recalled
another piece of information the Arch Priest had left her with - many
monsters in the Mazes of Menace were fearful of inscriptions of the
name of
the elven deity Elbereth. There was a layer of dust on the street, so
with
great haste she traced the name in the dust several times in rapid
succession.

She looked up to see that the warg was nowhere in sight, and took this
as a
sign of success and a good verification of that particular rumour.

After a brief pause over the mythical inscription to recover her
wounds,
she took her revenge on the warg.

Presently, another gnome lord appeared; on the gnome lord's hat the
number
'109' was inscribed. Was this the work of the other adventurer that
had
caused such mayhem in town?

The gnome lord died as quickly as his brethren had, and Essie looked
down
the street where she had first seen the stone giant to see it empty
save
for one who appeared to be a member of the town watch. She strolled
down
the street and came across a surprise - there was a temple in the
town, and
it was devoted to her own Loki!

Slightly less pleasant of a surprise was that the stone giant had
taken
refuge in the temple, but this nevertheless brought a smile to her
face as
she realised that the moment she set foot in the temple the stone
giant
would be struck with fear by the nature of the alignment of her power
with
that of the temple's.

She stopped in the doorway - as the priest greeted her, she beheld the
strange sight of a ghost floating over a gravestone in the corner of
the
temple, and a large pile of cursed scrolls, spellbooks, potions,
rings, and
even a tinning kit at her feet in the doorway. Here, then, was the
deceased
adventurer - from the angle she was at, she could read the name of the
newly deceased as 'csaiph0', but not the cause of death. It would be
simple
enough to decurse the items later on given the right preparations, so
she
gathered them up and set foot in the temple proper.

Sure enough, as she strode up to the giant, he turned and attempted to
flee. At the same time, the ghost of the former adventurer sailed away
through the temple wall, leaving the grave free for inspection. It
read,

"csaiph0, killed by a death ray."

Essie glanced down again at her magical armour - she would be in grave
danger were it not for that stroke of luck.

Strewn about the grave was a large assortment of goods. Of interest to
her
were the three wands, a piece of cloth large enough to be worn as a
cloak,
and an amulet, the latter of which had managed to escape the curse
befalling
most of the rest of the items. Before she had much of an opportunity
to
experiment with them, however, the stone giant strode up to the grave
and
grabbed many of the goods that were sitting there.

She attempted to hit the stone giant with her mace, but it did not
seem to
be capable of inflicting any significant harm. So, instead, she
employed
her other natural weapon - her feet.

It took some time, but Essie did manage to kick the stone giant to
death.

Finally, she donned the amulet and became aware of a large number of
monsters that had surrounded the temple. Apparently, this was a
medallion
of extrasensory perception.

She glanced over at the altar, and noticed a cursed pick-axe sitting
atop
it. Deciding this was better than nothing, she used one of her vials
of
holy water to remove the curse from it, and promptly set to work
opening
a new back entrance to the temple to avoid the crowd that had
gathered.

Being the tricker sort, she didn't think Loki would mind.

----

With pick-axe in hand, Essie ventured back to the passage where she
had
left the kitten. The kitten, recognising Essie, bolted towards here
only to
step on the teleportation trap and disappear.

Essie sighed, realising this would happen again if she tried to leave
the
mines right now, with or without the kitten, seeing as the trap was
blocking the passageway. Thus, she set to enlarging the passageway
with her
new pick-axe.

Deciding she had lasted this long just fine without the aid of the
kitten,
she departed through the new passage and left the mines altogether.

----

Essie's next objective was to seek the treasure of Sokoban, so - to
the
best of her knowledge - she was looking for an upwards staircase
somewhere
further down in the Dungeons of Doom.

It was not long before she found the other downwards passage on the
same
level as the mine entrance. The descent from there was largely
uneventful.
Along the way she procured a horn, which would be quite usable as a
musical
instrument of greater complexity than the bell she had begun with.

On the following floor she stumbled across a general store staffed by
one
Wonotobo. She remembered a piece of advice the Arch Priest had offered
her
- those that were crazy enough to set up shop in such a dangerous
locale
were both well-equipped and very knowledgeable about the value of
items
present in their environment.

Thus, Essie showed the shopkeeper a pair of scrolls for which he
offered
20 gold pieces, and deduced from that and what the Arch Priest had
previously told her that they were identification scrolls. Employing
one
of her remaining vials of holy water to bless the scrolls, she read
the
scrolls and directed the magic primarily at her other scrolls, rings,
and
wands. The magical power informed her that amongst what she carried,
she
had a cursed scroll of enchant armour, a cursed ring of regeneration,
a
cursed wand of opening, and a cursed wand of secret door detection.

After asking the shopkeeper for the value of some of her other
possessions
- primarily potions - she set out again in search of the entrance to
Sokoban.

----

It was another three dungeon levels before Essie came across the
entrance
she sought, by which time her knapsack was overflowing with items. She
hoped desperately to herself that the mythical bag of holding awaited
her
at the other end.

She set foot on the first level and looked around quizzically - there
was a
line of pits standing between her and the exit, and boulders large
enough
to fill the pits arranged in a puzzling fashion. It was obviously the
intent of whoever had designed this puzzle to maneuver the boulders in
such
a fashion as to place them in the pits to be able to reach the exit.

Fortunately, it was simple enough to arrange the boulders in the
requisite
fashion. In the process, she came across two scrolls stashed in a
corner;
they bore identical labels. She inferred that this had something to do
with
the presence of the boulders, thinking that they would be scrolls of
earth.

Eventually, she filled all of the pits blocking her from the exit and
strode up the stairs, eagerly looking around for the promised
treasure, and
was disappointed to see another set of boulders and pits.

Essie grumbled. "Fine," she said. "There can only be so many of these
puzzles that will fit between here and the open air. Let's do this."

One round of boulder-pushing later, Essie stood in front of the door
on the
far side of the former line of pits. Her medallion of ESP informed her
that
several hostile creatures were present on the other side, barring the
way
to the stairs, but there was nothing that she couldn't handle, and she
found herself facing a third round of boulder pushing and pit-filling.

Amongst the creatures that awaited her on the other side of the third
floor was a swarm of creatures she recognised as having a fearsome
reputation - soldier ants. Although she employed judicious use of
taking
Elbereth's name in vain in the process, she did, eventually, prevail.

Upon climbing the third set of stairs, she looked around with a sense
of
familiarity. The design of the room she saw matched one of the sets of
sketches the Arch Priest had shown her of the entrance to the resting
place
of the great treasure of Sokoban - it was the set hypothesising that
the
treasure was the eagerly anticipated bag of holding.

Thus it was that after one final round of boulder-pushing and one
large but
dull battle against a room full of assorted hostile creatures, Essie
emptied most of her backpack into a newfound bag of holding.

----

The next object that Essie desired was the luckstone said to be at the
end
of the Gnomish Mines. Thus, Essie ventured back upwards to the mines,
stopping only to run the value of some items past the storekeeper she
had
previously met.

It was at this stop that she definitively identified one of the wands
she
carried, from previous experimentation with trying to write with the
wand
coupled with the value quoted to her by the shopkeeper, as a wand of
make
invisible. She immediately turned the wand on herself and vanished
from
view, though she was still able to sense herself through the ESP
medallion.

----

As Essie wandered back through the first level of the mines, she
stumbled
across the kitten she had abandoned previously. It no longer seemed to
recognise her, so she threw the kitten a tripe ration she had come
across,
which it devoured hungrily before nuzzling up against her in
gratitude.

With the kitten in tow, Essie ventured back to Minetown, much more
easily
ab le to fight her way through the swarm of monsters that had
gathered.
Thus, she was able to take more of a leisurely look around than she
had
seen previously.

Amongst the stores that were present in Minetown was a lighting store.
Inside were several lamps (though none, she noted with dismay, that
seemed
to match the magic lamp she had found so soon in her expedition) and a
total of seven candles.

Essie thought back to the major piece of intelligence that the Arch
Priest
had provided her on the supposed location of the Amulet of Yendor -
that it
was held in Moloch's Sanctum at the very bottom of the Mazes of
Menace, and
that the entrance to the Sanctum was only passable by performing a
ritual
involving the Candelabrum of Invocation, the Bell of Opening, and the
Book
of the Dead. The Candelabrum required seven candles, which might not
be
present when she finally found the artifact; this was a good
opportunity
for her to procure those candles, so she bought all seven of them that
were
available at the store.

Finding little else of interest, Essie set down the stairs into the
series
of mines below Minetown, once again abandoning the kitten (who had
somehow
lost itself in the town somewhere where it refused to follow Essie).

----

It was a couple of levels down that an interesting series of events
conspired to occur at once.

The Arch Priest had suggested to Essie that consuming the bodies of
creatures she suspected to be poisonous would allow her to build up a
resistance to most of the poisons present in the dungeon. Thus, it
seemed
to be a good idea to slay and eat a giant spider that she had come
across.

Of course, poison being poison, as she ate the spider she quickly
found
herself weakened to the point where carrying all of her equipment
became
difficult, though not excessively so; she did have a peculiar
sensation of
healthiness as she finished, however.

Shortly thereafter, a white unicorn galloped into view. Amongst the
teachings of Loki that of all of the unicorns, only black unicorns
were to
be considered true unicorns; legends also suggested that unicorn horns
had
miraculous curative properties, such as being able to restore strength
lost
to poisoning.

Thus, with the aid of a wand of magic missiles, Essie slew the white
unicorn and claimed its horn for her own. With the aid of the horn's
powers, Essie's strength returned to her.

Thus it was that Essie gained resistance to poison and the horn of a
unicorn.

----

It was another two levels before Essie descended into a dark area she
felt
must be the end of the mines; wandering through the central cavernous
area,
she sensed the presence of several mimics - the infamous "Mimic of the
Mines", if her guess was correct.

It was here that Essie encountered stone giants for the second time,
and
the first in what might be considered their natural habitat. By now,
she
had been able to figure out precisely where they would be vunerable to
strikes from her mace, and she was able to fell them without
significant
difficulty.

As she ventured through the cavern, she also had her first encouter
with a
more legendary creature: the dreaded cockatrice, said to be able to
turn
one to stone on contact. She had not, as yet, been able to procure a
pair
of gloves, so being able to turn that power on her enemies was out of
the
question for the moment; in any case, she was nimbly able to avoid
exposing
herself to the creature long enough to be at risk before managing to
slay
it.

Eventually, in one of the hidden closets, she came across a pile of
gems on
top of which was a small gray stone. Having been warned by the Arch
Priest
against confusing a luckstone with the infamously heavy loadstone, she
nudged the stone with her boot and was relieved to see it skitter a
distance away from where it had started, indicating that it was the
sought-after luckstone.

Curiously, the same closet also held a set of seven candles; she added
them
to her possessions alongside the set she had previously acquired in
Minetown.

Thus it was that Essie conquered the Gnomish Mines and made off with
its
treasured luckstone.

----

On the way back upstairs to Minetown, Essie encountered another pair
of
stone giants. She put her tinning kit to use on their dead bodies and
enjoyed a tin of stone giant meat.

Unfortunately for Essie, the curse from the tinning kit spread to the
tin
it had created, and she felt nauseous and confused. It was perhaps
this
confusion that led her to read one of the scrolls she believed was a
scroll
of enchant weapon - which, indeed, it was, but in her confusion she
misread
the scroll, and her mace was covered in a strange golden shield,
repairing
some corrosion that had been previously inflicted upon it.

After recovering her senses through use of the unicorn horn, Essie
made a
mental note of that process, in the event that she wished to repeat it
with
any other weapons she might procur or with items of armour she might
be
wearing should she procure any non-cursed scrolls of enchant armour.

----

Reuniting with the kitten, Essie strolled around Minetown idly,
pondering
her next course of action - her initial three objectives had been
accomplished, meaning she was reasonably ready to begin her pursuit of
the
invocation artifacts.

As she strolled past the temple to Loki, she spotted a Woodland-elf
lurking
in a nearby room carrying a broadsword of elven make. A mental
connection
clicked and she seized the broadsword from the elf to name it Orcrist,
and
in so doing eliminate the other unwanted possibility for a divine gift
from
Loki.

She glanced back at the temple, and reached for the wands she had
previously seen used for creating monsters.

----

In the process of spilling enough blood to satisfy Loki sufficiently
to be
able to obtain a new weapon, one of the creatures Essie encountered
proved
capable of changing its form. The kitten set upon the dead body upon
its
death, and suddenly found itself in the form of a fire giant. The
giant
proved eager to join in the slaughter.

In the process, several members of the town watch were slain, and
Essie was
able to procure a pair of gloves from them in the process.

She also encountered a Grey-elf who carried a wand of death; she slew
him
and took custody of the wand. On a whim, she carried the corpse to the
altar and performed an elven sacrifice, summoning the demon lord
Juiblex!
If memory served, Juiblex's primary form of attack was by engulfing an
unfortunate victim, causing them to fall severely ill - but Essie had
a
wand of digging and a unicorn horn, so a means to defeat him presented
itself.

Thus, she attacked him, and mere moments later, the fearsome demon
lord had
been vanquished.

It took some time, but eventually, upon the offering of a giant
beetle, a
runed broadsword appeared upon the altar, accompanied by an etheral
voice:

"Use my gift wisely!"

Thus it was that Essie procured the fabled black blade of disaster,
Stormbringer.

----

It was now time, Essie decided, to formally begin her search for the
three
artifacts that she would need to gain entry to Moloch's sanctum.

The rumours she had been briefed on suggested that the Candelabrum was
held
by Vlad the Impaler, whose tower was somewhere in the depths of
Gehennom
below the Dungeons of Doom, and that the Book of the Dead was in the
possession of the feared Wizard of Yendor, who also made his residence
somewhere in Gehennom; the location of the Bell of Opening was,
however, a
mystery to her.

The logical thing to do, then, was to progress downwards to the castle
that
supposedly guarded the entrance to the Valley of the Dead, itself a
gateway
to Gehennom, in the hopes that a clue to the Bell's location would
present
itself along the way. This also offered the advantage of allowing
Essie to
avail herself of the treasure purportedly guarded in the castle - a
wand
said to grant the wishes of those who use it.

A level down from the mind entrance, in the process of more thoroughly
exploring the level, Essie came across a store. Her bloodthirsty blade
savaged the shopkeeper, leaving the large selection of scrolls and
tomes
available for the taking. With some of her scrolls of identification,
she
therefore successfully identified the cloth she had taken from the
poor
adventurer in Minetown so long ago as a fabled cloak of magic
resistance.
Unfortunately, it still bore a curse; fortunately, amongst the scrolls
from
the store was a scroll of remove curse.

Thus it was that Essie successfully identified and claimed for her own
a
cloak of magic resistance.

----

Shortly thereafter, Essie encountered another cockatrice and
successfully
slew it. With the pair of gloves she had claimed from the city watch,
she
hefted its dead body and proceeded to decorate the balance of the
dungeon
level and the level below it with many new statues.

A level below that, having long used up her initial supply of holy
water,
she encountered a dwarf who was carrying another vial of the precious
substance. There was a fountain conveniently situated in the same
room,
which she used to dilute down several potions she saw no immediate use
for
into water, and blessed them with the vial she had procured, yielding
a
total of five bottles of now-holy water for her later use.

One bottle thereof was put to use immediately, blessing the seven
scrolls
of identify she had at that time and using them to identify several
more of
her possessions, amongst them a cursed scroll of genocide, cursed
rings of
searching and free action, and scrolls of magic mapping and gold
detection.

----

It was around this time that Essie decided to put some of the
substantial
amount of gold she had possessed towards a holy cause, and ventured
back to
Minetown. With the considerable donations that Essie made to the
priest, she
felt a sense of divine protection surrounding her.

With her load considerably lightened, Essie set back to her descent
into
the Dungeons of Doom.

----

Shortly after returning from the mines, Essie encountered a kobold
whose
corpse she was inspired to tin. Remembering the earlier experience
with
repairing her damage mace, she stripped down to wearing only the cloak
of
magic resistance that was normally her outermost garment before eating
the
tin, employing the resulting confusion to protect both the cloak and
her
bloodthirsty weapon. She also took the opportunity to employ her two
remaining scrolls of enchant weapon, though she did not, at the time,
have
knowledge of how effective the enchantment had been.

----

Essie continued her descent; on the same level where she had found the
entrance to Sokoban, while searching for an alternate route forward,
she
stepped onto a tile which led to the peculiar sensation of feeling
momentarily different. She concluded that she had found a polymorph
trap,
and that the cloak of magic resistance had protected her.

The kitten-turned-fire giant was still with her, so she beckoned him
forward before shoving him onto the polymorph trap. The fire giant
promptly
turned into a zruty.

Unsatisfied, Essie shoved the creature back onto the trap several
times
until he was in the form of a small black dragon! It was the first
time
Essie had ever beheld such a creature - and in very unusual
circumstances.
If she recalled correctly, black dragons had the power to disintegrate
their foes with their black breath - a very powerful asset to have.
Unfortunately, the pet dragon was likely too small to be able to
employ
that ability, but by feeding it the other denizens of the dungeon, he
would
presumably be able to grow to its full potential.

Thus, as Essie progressed throughout most of the next few floors of
the
dungeon, she let the kitten-turned-fire giant-turned-baby dragon do
much of
the fighting.

----

Three floors down from where Essie had procured a new form of pet, the
staircase led her into a very large room full of assorted creatures.
It was
easily the size of most of the previous floors she had been on, only
it was
one continuous space.

This, thought Essie, was a golden opportunity to train the dragon to
use
its disintegration breath.

Thus, Essie fought her way to a safe distance to watch what
transpired.

As it turned out, the battle was too much for the poor dragon to
endure,
and it died before it had the chance to use its power for the first
time.

There was, however, the thought of a saving grace - dragon meat was
said to
grant those who consumed it the power to resist the abilities of that
colour of dragon - but only if it was that of a fully grown dragon.

Essie fought her way back to where the once-kitten had fallen and
concluded
that - as she had hoped - the dragon had reached its full size before
being
slain.

She put the tinning kit to work, employing one of the fountains in the
room
to remove the curse from the resulting tin; one meal later, she had a
strange sense of firmness.

Thus it was that Essie gained and lost a black dragon and came out
with the
ability to resist disintegration.

----

On the way to the stairs of the large room, Essie spotted a scroll
with a
label she recognised - it was another scroll of genocide. She stashed
it
safely away in the bag of holding.

On the following level, Essie assailed a water nymph, and ate the
resulting
body, leaving her with a peculiar feeling of jumpiness.

----

As she trudged down the next set of stairs, Essie heard the voice of
the
Arch Priest somewhere in the back of her mind:

"Your help is urgently needed at the Great Temple! Look for a ...ic
transporter..."

Essie stopped for a moment. Based on her earlier conversations with
the
Arch Priest, the only reason she would be called back in such a
fashion is
if a significant clue or lead on the location of the Amulet or one of
the
invocation artifacts presented itself - and the only item she did not
yet
have a strong lead on was the Bell of Opening. Could it be that that
would
be why she was being summoned?

In any case, the last part, despite having cut out, suggested to her
that
somewhere on the level she would find a portal back to the Great
Temple,
where her journey had begun.

Indeed, in the very next room, she stumbled across the portal she had
merely guessed at, and found herself once more before the gates of the
Great Temple.

Immediately, she deduced that something was wrong - the temple should
not
have zombies trudging around outside its surroundings! Perhaps the
situation was more dire than she had thought.

Nevertheless, she thought it best to hold off pursuing the matter
further
until she had obtained the wand of wishing from the castle at the
gates of
the Valley of the Dead; she felt she would not be ready until then.

Thus, Essie stepped back into the portal to resume her descent through
the
Dungeons of Doom.

----

It was shortly thereafter that Essie realised the ramifications of
eating the
nymph earlier as she suddenly found herself in unfamiliar surroundings
without
warning - she had spontaneously teleported.

Although the idea of being able to spontaneously teleport was somewhat
appealing, it had the potential of being extremely aggravating without
the
ability to control her destination. From her knowledge of the denziens
of the
dungeon that she had seen so far, there were two creatures whose
bodies would
be able to impart that ability: the Wizard of Yendor - who was
obviously one
of a kind - and tengu.

Recalling the scrolls of genocide she had discovered previously, she
dug
through her bag of holding for the one she had pulled from the grave
of the
adventurer in Minetown and therefore bore a curse, recalling that
magical
items that bore curses normally had the opposite of the intended
effect if
that effect was intended to be benficial.

Thus, she read the cursed scroll, and summoned a crowd of tengu.

Unfortunately, of the three dead bodies that she was able to consume
as a
result of the exercise, none granted her the control she desired.

Glancing in the bag of holding at her other scroll of genocide, an
idea
possessed her - strange readings of curse removal scrolls were said to
randomly bless and curse objects, and combined with the large number
of
useless potions she was carrying around, the effect could create both
holy
and unholy water, the latter being needed to attempt another shuffle
with
tengu. Thus, finding fountains on a lower dungeon level, she produced
several bottles of water and returned through the magic portal; having
not
experienced any random teleportations, she guessed - correctly - that
the
effect did not occur in the presence of the Great Temple.

The end result of the exercise was three bottles of holy water, three
of
unholy water, and three that remained untouched by the effect of the
scroll.

Returning to the dungeons, Essie cursed the scroll of genocide and
summoned another host of tengu, but in vain - none of the bodies she
consumed yielded the desired effect.

Undeterred, Essie decided to continue her descent through the dungeon.

----

Several levels further down, Essie encountered a large quantity of
human soldiers, apparently having previously been disturbed from their
barracks. In an eerie coincidence, several of them bore numbers on
their helmets - just as she had seen with many of the denizens of
Minetown. Was this the work of another poor adventurer?

By employing a blindfold, she employed telepathy to sense the ghost of
another adventurer - or was it the same adventurer, somehow? The
ghost's
name was apparently csaiph0 - the same as the poor soul who met his
end
in Minetown. Was some strange resurrection ploy at play here?

Fortunately, even a crowd of soldiers did not pose much of a threat to
Essie at this point in time; she cut through the largely inexperienced
men with ease, and pocketed the small yet filling rations that most of
them carried to act as a food source she hoped would last her through
to whatever end the adventure would find.

She worked her way over to where she had sensed the ghost, and came
across a gravestone:

"csaiph0, killed by a bolt of lightning".

The only items she found of interest on this grave were several wands
of types she was well familiar with by now - striking, teleportation,
lightning, and magic missile.

Slightly disappointed that this was not as beneficial as the previous
grave she had stumbled upon, she continued on her way.

----

After stumbling across some additional identify scrolls slightly lower
down in the dungeon, Essie concluded that one of the rings she had
found was a ring of conflict, which she promptly donned in the hopes
of having her foes vanquish each other instead of targeting her. She
also verified that Stormbringer had been thrice enchanted.

----

Upon arriving at the following dungeon level, Essie spied a gigantic
humanoid across the room that could only be a titan. She realised from
this and from some of the stories largely related to her by the Arch
Priest that he had likely arrived in Medusa's domain, and had no known
way of crossing the waters that would be between him and the passage
downwards.

Fortunately, this did not mean she could progress further; if the
rumours were true, the knight Perseus had met his end at Medusa's
hands, and he presumably would have had some means of crossing the
water. Thus, Essie produced a wand of digging and used it to burrow
through to the next floor.

After a brief bit of exploration, she found the passage back to the
floor she had just left, and came face to face with Medusa, who was
asleep.

With another glance at her magical armour, she attacked Medusa with
Stormbringer - the armour protected her from her lethal gaze, and
Medusa was vanquished quickly.

Sure enough, the petrified Perseus was sitting at the head of the
stairs; breaking down the statue with her pick-axe allowed her to get
at a pair of boots which she surmised would allow her to cross the
water to return to the entry passageway.

Sensing a curse emanating from the boots, she blessed the three
bottles of water she had produced earlier in order to obtain more holy
water, and used one of the resulting bottles to remove the curse from
the boots in anticipation of needing to wear them later.

Thus prepared, Essie continued down, sensing she was nearing the
bottom of the Dungeons of Doom.

----

Two levels below Medusa's sanctum, Essie found herself in a narrow
passageway. She sensed that this was a maze, and decided to employ one
of the scrolls of magic mapping she had previously found, allowing her
to locate and navigate to the exit thereof with relative ease.

After navigating herself through another - much smaller - maze on the
following floor, Essie stood in front of the legendary castle guarding
the entrance to the Valley of the Dead. She sensed that the moat
surrounding the castle had several guardian monsters; after waiting
for the ring of conflict she was wearing to get the crowd of monsters
she sensed in the castle courtyard to thin itself out and for the
space in front of the drawbridge to be safe, she strode in front of
the drawbridge and produced the horn she had found previously.

After some experimentation, and listening carefully to the response of
the drawbridge mechanism to the tunes she played with the horn, she
found that playing the tune 'FDADG' opened the drawbridge and
portcullis.

Sensing a delicious opportunity for chaos, she waited for one of the
courtyard's guardians to step under the portcullis, and once again
played the passtune, smashing the creature under the portcullis and
killing it instantly. She then reopened the drawbridge and waited for
another such opportunity to present itself.

After a few such smashing cycles, however, one of the soldiers in the
castle produced a wand of striking and zapped it in the direction of
the drawbridge, causing it to collapse into the moat.

Undeterred at this loss of lethality, Essie merely stepped aside to
allow the ring of conflict to continue to work its magic.

Once the crowd had thinned sufficiently, she donned the boots she had
just retrieved from Perseus in order to cross the
now-otherwise-impassable moat and floated past the few remaining
stragglers into the throne room.

She saw two doors on the sides of the throne room and surmised based
on intelligence the Arch Priest had provided her with that the wand of
wishing would be at a room at either end of a hallway just beyond one
of those two doors. She thus fought her way into one of the corridors,
only to realise that both of those two rooms were devoid of the wand,
and progressed into the other corridor, where the room towards the
rear of the castle contained an inviting chest.

Locking the door behind her, Essie opened the chest to find a wand
within.

Thus it was that Essie procured the castle's fabled wand of wishing.

----

The most important thing to remember about a wand, Essie had heard,
was that it stored a limited amount of magical energy, which could be
replenished a limited number of times by the means of a scroll of
charging. Not all wands were receptive to being recharged, however,
and particularly powerful wands might only be able to be recharged
once reliably.

The wand of wishing was certainly powerful, and she wanted to be able
to extract as many wishes from it as possible; thus, recharging the
wand might be a necessity.

Thus, the first wish from the wand was for "2 blessed scrolls of
charging", and she received precisely that.

After some thought, Essie decided what she needed most at present was
a means of travelling quickly and a means of reliably carrying the
ever-increasing weight of the bag of holding.

Her next wish was therefore for "blessed fixed greased +2 speed
boots", to provide the former.

The next attempt to use the wand yielded nothing. Essie realised those
had probably been all of the initial charges of the wand, and resorted
to one of her scrolls of charging, which, theoretically, would provide
the wand with another full charge - she guessed this would be a
nominal three wishes.

The first wish of that set of three was "blessed fixed greased +2
gauntlets of power".

Her next wish required some thought, but she eventually settled upon
the acquision of an artifact with the power to save its wearer from
death a single time - a "blessed amulet of life saving".

For the last of the set of three, she decided upon a means to produce
scrolls at will, preserving the blessings (or curses) of the paper she
wrote them upon - an "uncursed magic marker".

Recalling that one last charge could occasionally be obtained from
worn-out wands, she stashed the wand and its potential remaining wish
into her bag of holding.

Using the newly acquired magic marker, Essie spent some time
enchanting her shirt and cloak, reaching a fourfold enchantment of the
shirt and a threefold enchantment of the cloak.

Thus prepared, Essie departed the castle.

----

Essie did not quite feel prepared to respond to whatever tip the Arch
Priest was willing to provide her with respect to the location of the
Bell of Opening, but she nevertheless decided to venture back to the
portal in the hopes that by the time she arrived back at the Great
Temple she would be ready to pursue it.

Three floors above the castle, Essie found herself back in Medusa's
sanctum. Navigating her way around the abode, she eventually found an
exit that opened onto a large open body of water; donning a blindfold,
she used her by-now-intrinsic powers of telepathy to determine that
several electric eels made their home therein.

In an effort to scare some of the eels away and thereby avert the
possibility of being dragged down to a watery grave, Essie attempted
to sound a warning with the horn - but instead, she was surprised to
see a cone of frost burst forth. The horn was, indeed, a horn of frost
all along.

Fortunately, an opportunity quickly presented itself to slip past the
eels, and Essie floated along above the water past several eels and
gremlins until she found a building on the far side of the waters she
surmised was her point of entry.

On the other side of the door were several gremlins, whom she swatted,
and a small moat between her and the staircase. Using one of the
scrolls of earth she had found in Sokoban, she filled part of the moat
in with a boulder and returned to the floor above.

----

On the way back to the magic portal, Essie chose to do some side
exploration in the efforts of honing her skills to the point where she
would feel reay to act on the Arch Priest's instructions. In the
progress of doing so, she came across another temple not far above
where Medusa's sanctum lay; although it was not a temple of Loki, she
nevertheless was inspired to hand most of her gold to the priest so as
to obtain further divine protection.

----

By the time Essie reached the portal again, Essie still did not quite
feel up to the task. Was it just a lack of self-confidence, or was
something else at play?

In a stroke of luck, however, on the far side of the portal she
quickly spotted a creature she had encountered previously - a wraith.
She knew first-hand that when such a creature left remains behind
after being destroyed, those remains would impart great insight and
experience if consumed.

Not willing to risk that chance in front of the gates to the Great
Temple, Essie lured the ghost back to the portal and promptly
destroyed it - and, indeed, the creature did leave behind remains.

One insubstantial meal later, Essie felt prepared to speak to the Arch
Priest once more.

----

"Ah, SGrunt, my daughter--"

"Oh, are even you calling me that now?" Essie sneered. "What have you
been up to in the short time that I've been away?"

"You have returned to us at last," droned the Arch Priest, apparently
completly ignoring Essie's words as though she wasn't speaking at all.

"Like I said, it hasn't been that long since I last set foot here. Now
why did you summon me back here? I was under the impression that--

"A great blow has befallen our order," continued the Arch Priest, in
a typical Arch Priest drone. "Perhaps you can help us."

"I thought I already was," said Essie, "by taking on this insane quest
in the first place."

The acolytes surrounding the altar pointed and whispered at her. Too
late, Essie remembered she wasn't supposed to speak of why she had
suddenly disappeared.

"First, however, I must determine if you are prepared for this great
challenge," the Arch Priest said.

Essie glared at him. "Couldn't you have done that before sending me
away in the first place?"

There was a pause. The High Priest seemed to be concentrating his
attention on Essie.

"Why are you staring at me like that, anyway?" she said. "I'm not
going to spontaneously start on fire or anything."

The High Priest blinked. Then he smiled.

"Yes, SGrunt--"

"Don't call me that!"

"You are truly ready now. Attend to me and I shall tell you of what
has transpired..."

"Oh, here we go again."

----

One short tale of destruction and chaos later, Essie had been informed
of the presence of Nalzok, a senior minion of Moloch himself, in the
vicinity of the temple, where he had made off with the Mitre of
Holiness.

"You never really did much with that stuffy old helmet, though, did
you?" said Essie, after the Arch Priest had finished his spiel. "Why
should
I care about getting it back?"

"Oh, did I forget to mention? We also noticed that he was carrying the
Bell of Opening."

Essie stared at him. "You know, you could have just said that to begin
with, and I wouldn't have needed to listen to that lecture.

"Well, fine. Maybe the fastest way to get off this whole 'quest'
business is just to get it over with. I'll go get this bell of yours,
and maybe that helmet will come in useful too."

She turned and set off for the temple's cellar, which apparently led
to a series of catacombs which would deposit her near Nalzok's
hideout.

----

As it transpired, the catacombs had been infested with many of
Nalzok's minions. These undead proved easy enough to deal with, but
hindered easy location of the passage onwards. Essie did, eventually,
manage to find that passage, and climbed out into a large graveyard.
At the centre of the graveyard was a cloud of darkness; this was
obviously, then, the Temple of Nalzok.

Surrounding the temple was a larger crowd of undead creatures than she
had ever seen before. Even her ring of conflict did not prove
tremendously useful in thinning out such a large crowd.

Essie had previously realised that if she concentrated in the correct
fashion, she could initiate a random teleportation of her own will -
perhaps this was how the power was intended to be used all along, and
the randomness was merely a side effect.

Thus, she lured the crowd of undead away from the temple, and managed,
after a few attempts, to teleport to the other side of the crowd,
leaving the passage between her and the entrance to the temple clear.
Making her way past the swarm of ghosts that nevertheless remained
inside, she descended into the depths of the temple.

----

Two levels of catacombs were the next challenge that Essie faced,
though they were only challenging insofar as the exits were well
hidden amongst the handful of undead creatures that resided therein.

In the process, she was able to consume the remains of another wraith.
Shortly thereafter, she realised that she had developed a sense for
nearby creatures with hostile intent, though she was only able to
determine how much of a challenge they were likely to pose as opposed
to what they were.

----

Shortly thereafter, Essie stumbled across the exit from the second
level of catacombs down into a lair filled with boiling lava. Sure
enough, Nalzok was visible within his lair.

Immediately, Essie reached for a wand of fire and burned 'Elbereth'
into the floor at the foot of the staircase; she had hit upon this as
being far more permanent than most of the other means of writing.

This caught Nalzok's attention, and suddenly he was at the edge of the
stairs. "Ah, so the Arch Priest has sent another acolyte to retrieve
the
Mitre of Holiness," he taunted.

"Acolyte?" said Essie. "I think not."

"No, I see you are no acolyte. Perhaps I shall have some fun today
after
all--"

Nalzok glimped the text burned onto the floor and panicked. Though he
managed to summon a group of minions, the exercise was nevertheless
over
very quickly, as Nalzok's body dissolved into a cloud of noxious gas.

As the demon dissolved, an ominous voice echoed throughout the
chamber:

"Thou hast defeated the least of my minions. Know now that Moloch is
aware
of thy presence!"

Essie stared defiantly into the distance.

"Bring it, amulet-boy."

----

Thus it was that Essie retrieved the Bell of Opening and the Mitre of
Holiness. A short journey later, Essie once more found herself facing
the
Arch Priest.

The Arch Priest gave Essie an approving look. "You have returned,
SGrunt--"

"Don't call me that!"

"And with the Mitre of Holiness, I see," said the Arch Priest, as
though
pointedly ignoring the protest. "Congratulations."

"Right. No mention of the Bell? Because I have that too." Essie
resisted
the urge to ring the bell then and there, just to prove the point.

"I have been in meditation," continued the Arch-Priest, "and have
received
direction from a minion of Loki. Loki commands that you retain the
Mitre of
Holiness."

"Oh, really? Well, gee, thanks, a musty old helmet is really going to
be of
use here."

"With it, you must recover the Amulet of Yendor."

"Isn't that what I've been doing all along? I don't think I need the
help
of a silly hat to do that."

"Go forth, and let Loki guide your steps."

With that, the Arch Priest gave a dismissive wave.

"Hmph," said Essie. "We'll see about that."

----

One magic portal-assisted journey later - why couldn't she have used
that
method of travel to arrive at the Mazes of Menace in the first place -
Essie returned to the Dungeons of Doom.

With the bell of opening in hand, there was only one logical
destination
left, for it was there that those holding the Candelabrum of
Invocation and
the Book of the Dead resided; it was there that the ultimate objective
of
her quest, the Amulet of Yendor itself, resided. It was the land of
unspeakable crulty and harm, lying beyond the Valley of the Dead and
the
formerly well-guarded castle at its threshold.

Gehennom.

----

For the most part, the journey back to the castle was uneventful. Upon
arriving at the level of Medusa's sanctum, however, Essie found that
the
staircase had been surrounded by hostile creatures; undeterred, she
retreated to the level above and once again employed a wand of
digging,
this time twice in rapid succession to descend to the floor below.

Eventually, Essie once again stood before the castle, and employed the
other scroll of earth she had found in Sokoban to fill in the moat
before
the former site of the drawbridge to gain entry. If she understood the
position of the castle correctly, the entrance to the Valley of the
Dead
should be somewhere in the back area of the castle.

As she entered a passageway behind the former site of the throne (it
had
disappeared earlier when she had attempted to sit on it), a trap door
opened beneath her feet, and she was unceremoniously dumped downwards.
By the smell of burnt flesh and decay and the sound of groans and
moans,
she surmised that she had, indeed, reached the Valley of the Dead.

Essie quickly searched around to find a passage to return upwards, as
she
guessed she would later need to find her way back up to the entrance
to the
Dungeons of Doom from the depths of Gehennom. It was only a few steps
away
from where she had landed, and at the upper end she mysteriously found
herself in another small maze, with no sign of the passage she had
just
climbed from.

Presently she found herself staring at the rear gate of the castle,
and
immediately fell back through another trap door to return to the
Valley.

Though the Valley, true to its name, housed a large number of undead
creatures - along with some demons - there was nothing of note that
she had
not previously encountered somewhere, particularly in her efforts to
retrieve the Bell of Opening. It was therefore not much longer before
she
arrived at a temple to Moloch, in the back of which was a
not-too-carefully-hidden, trap-lined passageway which culminated in
the
gate to Gehennom itself.

Thus it was that Essie fought her way through the Valley of the Dead
and
descended into Gehennom.

----

Immediately upon arriving in the maze that apparently comprised the
first
level of Gehennom proper, Essie put on a blindfold so as to employ
telepathy to identify any major foes she might be facing. She
immediately
picked out the presence of the demon prince Asmodeus.

Undeterred, she set about exploring the maze, determining that the
entrance
to Asmodeus' lair was probably at the far end of the maze.

After exploring much of the maze, however, she eventually stepped on a
trap
door and fell to the level below.

In the maze that followed, after some searching she was able to locate
the
passage upwards, but teleported away before she was able to make use
of it.
Sure enough, before reaching that passage, she eventually stepped on
another trap door and fell not one, not two, but three levels down,
landing
at what telepathy showed to be Baalzebub's lair.

Fortunately, she had landed only a few steps from the passage upwards,
and
after some effort, she managed to work her way up through the next
three
levels without too much difficulty, and she ascended the stairs into
the
heart of Asmodeus' lair.

Perhaps due to her peculiar style of money management, when the demon
prince appeared before Essie, he demanded a single gold piece for
'safe
passage'. Considering that she had been on both sides of the lair and
did
not see any passage that could be remotely considered safe, she
naturally
refused. This seemed to offend Asmodeus, and he immediately assailed
her,
though a whack with Stormbringer forced him to disappear to somewhere
else.

Thinking he might be attempting to flee, Essie began fighting her way
out
of the lair, only for Asmodeus to appear again and attempt to attack
her.
Yet only a few moments later, Asmodeus reappeared and once again
attempted
to assail her; he fled once more upon being hit with Stormbringer.

So it was that, as Essie maneuvered through Asmodeus' lair from exit
to
entrance, she was set upon by the demon prince every couple of metres
without any sign of him giving up.

With the aid of the pick-axe, as had been the case with the mazes she
had
previously ventured through, she eventually found her way back to the
upwards passage, and, indeed, Asmodeus was sitting there, apparently
attempting to recover his strength.

Remembering her 'battle' with Nalzok, Essie lured Asmodeus from the
stairway, claiming the space for herself and using a wand of fire to
burn
the name Elbereth at the foot of the stairs. Sure enough, even the
mighty
demon prince Asmodeus panicked upon the sight.

During the course of the battle, however, one of Asmodeus' minions
managed
to employ a wand of digging to punch a hole through the floor next to
the
staircase, and in true cowardly fashion Asmodeus took the opportunity
to
flee downards through it.

Although she followed him down below, she was unable to locate where
the
demon prince had fled to.

----

Presently, Essie decided to continue onwards in the hopes of locating
the
domain of one of the two remaining artifact holders.

Returning to the level of Baalzebub's lair, she was inspired by her
previous demon battles to burn 'Elbereth' into the floor at the foot
of the
upwards stairs. She then assaulted the lair proper, finding
surprisingly
little resistance until she found herself facing Baalzebub.

In an eerily familiar scenario, when Baalzebub appeared, he demanded
one
gold piece for 'safe passage', and Essie naturally refused; Baalzebub
attacked, she hit him with Stormbringer, and he fled. Having been
blinded
moments earlier, she definitively saw him flee to the upwards passage.

Thus, Essie battled her way back to the stairs and took advantage of
the
resulting fear on Baalzebub's part to vanquish the demon prince once
and
for all.

The level below appeared to be a large swamp; she surmised this is
where
Juiblex had been summoned from in the ritual she had performed so long
ago
in Minetown. Her levitation boots made for a simple and uneventful
journey
above the marshes.

----

Several floors below after some lengthy maze explorations and the
eventual
use of a handful of scrolls of magic mapping (courtesy of the magic
marker), Essie detected the presence of the demon prince Orcus.
Remembering
her approach in dealing with Asmodeus, she dug down to the floor
below.

Using another scroll of magic mapping on that floor, however, located
two
sets of upward stairs. Picking one at random, she found that the
stairs led
into a tower, and judging by the general decor of the environment and
the
lack of heat and smoke, she had found the domain of Vlad the Impaler.

Although several monsters had made their home in the tower, she was
easily
able to cut them down, and as she searched through some of the closets
and
rooms, she came across an amulet identical to the one she was wearing
- an
amulet of life saving. She, naturally, decided to hold on to it.

It was not long before Essie reached the top floor of the tower and
came
face to face with Vlad the Impaler himself. At a glance, he did not
appear
to be that imposing to the now-veteran warrior, and she thought to
herself,

"For all that matters, I could probably beat him to death with this
thoroughly rusty key without imperiling myself."

It was a silly thought, but she took it up as a challenge, and
brandished
the key at the lord of vampires.

As it turned out, Essie was quite correct in that assessment - it was
not
long before Vlad fell before the mighty thoroughly rusty key, earning
it
the title of Vladsbane. Clutched in his once-again-dead hands was a
seven-branched candelabrum, to which Essie quickly attached seven of
her
fourteen candles.

Thus it was that Vlad the Impaler fell, and Essie obtained the
Candelabrum
of Invocation.

----

It was not long before Essie had scaled down the tower and set out for
the
other set of stairs, emerging in the midst of Orcus and his entourage.
As
with the other demon princes, a hit with Stormbringer provoked him to
flee
to the upwards staircase, leaving Essie in the midst of several
vampires
and shades.

Essie promptly realised she would not be able to attack the shade with
any
of her conventional techniques; her weapons simply passed through
them. She
realised, however, that she did have an item made of silver that would
be
capable of harming them.

Thus it was that the Bell of Opening found a use that had never been
intended for it, easily searing the shades to death.

Essie quickly set to carving a route from the passage down to the
passage
up, being set upon by Orcus and his wand of death for much of the
route,
albeit to a much lesser extent than his fellow demon nobility. When
she
reached the upstairs, however, Orcus chose to flee upwards, and when
she
pursued him, he simply fled upstairs again.

Fortunately, on the level she had now arrived at, the two passages
were
mere steps from each other, and she was able to beat Orcus to the
stairs
this time, preventing his escape; she burned 'Elbereth' into the foot
of
the stairs once more, and mere moments later Orcus had fallen before
Stormbringer.

Thus it was that the demon prince Orcus was slain, and his wand of
death
passed into the hands of Essie.

----

Several short trips downwards later, Essie descended below the level
where
she had found the entrance to Vlad's tower. A telepathic scan revealed
to
her that she had, at last, found the hiding place of the Wizard of
Yendor,
holder of the last of the three invocation artifacts - the Book of the
Dead.

As she explored, however, she quickly realised there was no way to
enter
the tower from the part of the maze she was in; perhaps there was an
entrance on a lower level.

Finding the stairs rather quickly, she descended to telepathically
sense a
veritable zoo of monsters who were apparently on a lower floor of the
Wizard's tower. She did not, however, find an entrance to that floor -
or
to the floor of the tower she detected on the next level below, for
that
matter.

The next floor bore no sign of a tower nor any other notable features.
Perhaps, belonging to a Wizard as it did, the tower could only be
entered
through magical means?

Essie decided to keep descending through the mazes - it would be
desirable
to find the location where she was supposed to perform the invocation
ritual before assaulting a tower that could be protected in such a
fashion.

----

A few levels down, Essie stumbled across a curious structure
surrounded
by a moat, resembling a miniature tower. She sensed in the tower two
liches
and a vampire lord; perhaps they were protecting something magical?

She stood at the edge of the moat and zapped a wand of digging across,
opening a hole in the side of the tower. She then employed her horn of
frost to create an icy bridge across while simultaneously blasting the
inhabitants with freezing cold.

Essie stepped into the centre of the tower to assault the tower's
remaining
inhabitans, and instead felt the familiar sensation of activating a
magic
portal, suddenly finding herself several floors up in the bottom floor
of
the Wizard's tower. So this was the means of entry?

In any case, she decided to stick with her original plan of finding
the
location for the invocation ritual before proceeding with her assault
on
the Wizard's tower and the Wizard himself, sensing that he would prove
to
be a more dangerous foe than either of his artifact-possessing
compatriots
had been.

----

It was another four levels below this miniature tower, on the fiftieth
level of the Mazes of Menace, that Essie eventually hit rock bottom -
she
was unable to locate any further passageways, and attempting to dig
one
eventually yield a strange material that was too hard to dig through.
After
a great deal of searching, she located a position, however, where the
floor
vibrated strangely under her feet.

In the absence of any other clues, she surmised that this was the
location
where the invocation was to take place.

She dug out a small area around the location, dropped a single piece
of
gold on top of it, and used one of her otherwise thoroughly-underused
scrolls of light to illuminate the area so that she would remember
precisely where she needed to go when the time was right.

It was time, then, to go and retrieve the Book of the Dead.

----

Returning to the miniature tower that served as the gateway to the
Wizard's
main tower, she stepped once more through the magic portal to find
herself
on the first floor of the miniature dungeon.

Finding the creatures present to be of minimal difficulty, she fought
through a beehive and came across a devil who possessed another wand
of
death, which she wrestled away from its dead body. Slightly deeper on
the
first floor, she found another miniature tower.

After killing the creatures in the moat - two fearsome kraken who
proved
too slow to react to her attacks as well as two eels - she breached
the
tower in exactly the same fashion as the first - by zapping a wand of
digging across the moat followed by use of the horn of frost to bridge
the
moat and harm the inhabitants.

After killing the lich and vampire lord, she clambered up a ladder in
the
centre of the tower to arrive on the second floor.

Several empty passages later, Essie opened the door to reveal a large
crowd
of monsters, largely asleep, though they awoke rather quickly when the
ring
of conflict's magic began to kick in and the crowd began tearing
itself
apart.

Once the crowd had thinned sufficiently, she dashed through the
remnants to
the passages on the other side, and eventually found another ladder
upwards.

After searching through some more passages on the third floor, Essie
eventually found an entrance to another area containing a miniature
tower.
She knew within dwelled the Wizard himself, who would require special
care
to deal with. Taking no chances, she allowed some time for the eels
and
kraken in the moat to either kill each other or float away from the
nearest
wall of the tower, then fired a wand of digging across the moat to
open a
hole in the wall, followed by a death ray from the wand she had first
found
in Minetown.

Even the mighty Wizard of Yendor did not last very long against a
death
ray.

Using the horn of frost to cross the moat, Essie found a papyrus
spellbook
resting on the floor where the Wizard had existed only moments
previously.

Thus it was that Essie slew the Wizard of Yendor and claimed the Book
of
the Dead, the last of the three invocation artifacts.

----

Essie quickly raced back down through the tower, through the magic
portal,
and to the vibrating patch of floor she had located previously.

Recalling the holy rituals she had performed previously in her duties
as an
acolyte, she remembered the general steps that would be necessary for
the
ceremony she believed she was required to perform.

Firstly, she lit the candles of the Candelabrum of Invocation. The
candles
burned eerily brightly.

Secondly, she rung the Bell of Opening, which issued an unsettling
shrill
sound.

Finally, with some light to read by and the attention of the spirits
called
by the Bell of Opening, she began to recite from the Book of the Dead.

As she read, the floor began to shake violently, and the walls
surrounding
her location began to bend and crumble. Suddenly, Essie found herself
surrounded by a wide moat, several fire traps, and standing at the top
of a
stairwell leading down.

This was the moment of truth - this was undoubtedly the entrance to
Moloch's Sanctum.

----

The entry hall to the Sanctum gave way to a series of passages, not
unlike
some of those Essie had explored in the Wizard's tower. The first
point of
interest she found was a large graveyard, but dealing with those was
almost
second nature to her by now given the supposed horrors she had
experienced
while searching for the Bell of Opening - she let the ring of conflict
do
most of the work, and once the crowd had thinned sufficiently she
dashed
through to find another passage at the other end of the graveyard.

She sensed a large number of hostile creatures at the end of this
passageway, so she locked the doorway behind her so as to only need to
deal
with creatures on one front. On the other side of the new doorway,
however,
she encountered not monstrous creatures, but priests and priestesses
of the
dread god Moloch himself. It was not long before one employed a wand
of
striking to destroy the door behind him, forcing the issue;
fortunately the
ring of conflict was able to thin out the crowds without too much
additional difficulty.

On the other side, she spied a temple surrounded by fire traps, and
sensed
the presence of the High Priest of Moloch within; given the sensitive
nature of the location, she surmised that the High Priest held the
Amulet
of Yendor.

Employing the nearly forgotten wand of secret door detection she had
recovered from the adventurer in Minetown, she eventually located the
hidden entrance to the temple not too far from where the passage she
had
entered through joined the chamber.

A barbed devil emerged from the temple through the newly-detected
door,
whom she slew without difficulty, leaving only the High Priest to deal
with.

She stepped through the fire trap to the doorway and was greeted with
a
brusque voice: "Infidel, you have entered Moloch's Sanctum! Be gone!"

The High Priest of Moloch stepped angrily towards her, brandishing a
mace
which had fused with his hand.

Essie fired a death ray at the High Priest.

As the death ray flew towards his High Priest, the voice of Moloch
boomed
through the temple: "Thou shalt suffer!"

From out of nowhere, a bolt of lightning whizzed past Essie, blinding
her
with the flash, but leaving her otherwise unharmed.

Inevitably, the death ray hit the High Priest.

Thus passed the High Priest of Moloch.

Using a unicorn horn to recover her vision, Essie quickly searched the
remains of the High Priest and discovered a strange amulet, which
resisted
her effort to place it into her bag of holding.

Thus it was that Essie defeated Moloch's holiest and recovered the
Amulet
of Yendor.

----

A sense of urgency filled Essie - she had retrieved the object of her
quest, and it was now time to depart as quickly as possible!

She raced back to the entrance of the Sanctum and back up the stairs,
using
the frost horn to cross the moat that had been created in the process
of
the invocation, and began the slow process of climbing back up from
the
bottom of the Mazes of Menace back to its very top.

Less than ten steps past the edge, a figure suddenly appeared in front
of
her, accompanied by a booming voice:

"So thou thought thou couldst kill me, fool."

She beheld the figure of the Wizard of Yendor, looking very much
alive.

In her rush, her first instinct was to keep moving, and Stormbringer
took
that as a cue to attack the Wizard - after several blows the Wizard
disappeared, apparently having fled back to the stairs, in a fashion
very
reminiscent of the demon princes she had battled.

It was not long after that the Wizard was slain for the second time.

----

Essie quickly climbed up seven levels from where she had performed the
invocation ritual; upon trying to climb the set of stairs from the
eighth,
she was mysteriously forced away from the stairs and found herself at
the
end of the hallway where the stairs resided. She was able to ascend
the
stairs the secomd time without incident.

Four levels above Orcus' lair, the Wizard of Yendor made another
appearance, and disappeared just as quickly at the hands of another
death
ray.

No further incidents occured until she attempted to leave Baalzebub's
lair,
six more levels above; when she did so, she suddenly found herself
back in
Juiblex's swamp, the level below the lair. She quickly crossed the
swamp
again and was able to depart Baalzebub lair without incident.

Essie was again teleported from the stairs two levels above
Baalzebub's
lair, and though she was able to leave that level for the second time
without incident, attempting to enter Asmodeus' lair from the level
above
instead dropped her back down to that same level, necessitating that
she
navigate it a third time.

When she re-entered Asmodeus' lair, the demon prince proved to still
be
very much alive - perhaps he had fled back up the stairs when she
sought
him out earlier. Repeating a familiar pattern, she attempted to
navigate
back to the stairs while being set upon by Asmodeus every few steps.

Unfortunately, as Essie approached the stairs, she fell through the
same
hole that Asmodeus had originally fled through.

To her surprise, Asmodeus followed her through the hole! Fortunately,
she
did not land too far from the stairs, and she was able to beat him
there.
With a few more strokes of Stormbringer from the stairs, Asmodeus,
too,
fell.

Winding her way around Asmodeus' lair, Essie once again fell afoul of
the
mysterious force upon attempting to leave Gehennom altogether, though
she
only found herself a short distance from the stairs a result. On her
second
attempt, she found herself once more on the level below, just on the
other
side of the down stairs from where the up stairs were.

She quickly returned to Asmodeus' lair and wound her way through and
around
the residence once more, and this time the mysterious force permitted
her
to exit Gehennom.

Thus it was that Essie returned from Gehennom with the Amulet of
Yendor.

----

The balance of the ascent through the Valley, the castle, Medusa's
lair,
and the rest of the Dungeons of Doom was surprisingly uneventful,
marred
only by the occasional spontaneous teleport, and Essie soon found
herself
standing at the foot of the stairs that had first brought her into the
Dungeons of Doom.

She had a sense, however, that her journey was not yet over. Sensing
that
she would soon need to locate magical portals, she wrote out several
scrolls of gold detection, recalling that mispronouncing the scrolls
could
be used to detect traps and, by extension, portals.

Indeed, as she attempted to climb the stairs, she was greeted by a
mysterious voice:

"Well done, mortal! But now thou must faice the final Test... Prove
thyself
worthy or perish!"

Her vision blurred, and she found herself in a dark cavern, greeted by
an
unpleasantly familiar voice:

"So thou thought thou couldst kill me, fool."

She once again set upon the newly-revived Wizard with Stormbringer,
and
made two observations in the process.

First, he would occasionally flee as though there was an escape area
somewhere nearby.

Second, an earth elemental had detached itself from the wall of the
cavern.

Was this, perhaps, the Plane of Earth? Would the escape then be a
magical
portal?

She attempted to recall a long-forgotten reading of one of the
spellbooks
she had first entered the dungeon, confusing her, and allowing her to
misread one of the scrolls of gold detection she had prepared - and,
indeed, on the far side of the plane existed a magical portal.

After finishing off the Wizard, she drew several wands of digging and
proceeded to cut her way through the tough rock to reach the portal.

When she stepped through the portal, she found herself floating in a
strange cloudy area with several flying creatures, including air
elementals
- the Plane of Air? Taking note of the lack of gravity and the ease
with
which the other creatures were able to maneuver, she donned her
levitation
boots and was able to gain control over her movements.

Employing another scroll of gold detection, she located the portal on
the
far side of the clouds and set out towards it. Along the way, an air
elemental assailed her, but she was able to retrieve a long-forgotten
wand
of opening, originally pulled from the poor adventurer in Minetown,
and
utilise it to free herself from its confines.

Soon, Essie found herself on what was likely the Plane of Fire. Here,
reading the gold detection scroll yielded, in addition to the portal,
a
great number of - what else? - fire traps.

She was able to float over to the portal largely without incident.

By process of elimination, Essie correctly guessed that on the other
side
of the portal would be the Plane of Water. She found herself in a
bubble
surrounded by an immense quantity of water, and spied other bubbles
churning around it; in the water proper she sensed water elementals
and
other sea creatures.

Here attempting to locate the portal yielded only an instantaneous
result,
though after some floating around Essie located the bubble where the
portal
resided.

What would be on the other side of this portal?

----

The natural answer, of course, would be her ultimate destination - the
mythical Astral Plane, where the High Temples of the gods were locates
-
and that is indeed where the portal took her. The idea, of course, was
that
by finding the high altar of Loki, she would be able to present the
Amulet
to Him directly.

As Essie appeared, a strange voice boomed, "Thy desire for conflict
shall
be fulfilled!" and four hostile angels appeared around her,
accompanied
momentarily by a master lich.

After a few moments of battle, the master lich eventually summoned
several
creatures, including - to his ultimate peril - a cockatrice, who
remained
intact when Essie slew it.

Several statues later, Essie took stock of her situation. There were
three
temples - presumably one of the three would be the Temple of Loki. She
decided to start with the central temple, which was directly in front
of
her.

The chamber on the other side of the door contained several priests,
priestesses, angelic beings, and a mysterious black hooded figure.
Scanning
the figure with telepathy suggested its identity as one of the Riders
of
the Apocalypse - Famine.

In any case, the chamber quickly found itself filled with statues, and
Essie quickly evaded Death to maneuver towards the altar proper.

Disappointingly, the altar was that of Odin.

She maneuvered around Famine back to the entry hallway and proceeded
to the
rightmost temple, guarded by Death and an accompaniment of priests and
angels.

After creating several more statues, Essie found herself standing on
the
altar of Tyr.

An idea crossed her mind to use the residual wish from the castle's
wand
for a mythical helm of opposite alignment, which would have allowed
her to
offer the Amulet to Tyr instead, but in the heat of the moment she
decided
that attempting to do so would take too much time.

She therefore maneuvered back out of the temple, making a close brush
with
Death in the process - just enough to feel a part of her life force
draining away.

By process of elimination, the temple of Loki must have been the one
on the
lefthand part of the plane.

As she maneuvered back there, she bumped into Famine, who refused to
turn
into a status. She stepped back and employed a wand of teleportation
to put
him at her back and quickly ran off, only to run directly into a
priestess;
weak as he was from his brush with Famine, she suffered greatly at the
hands of a spell the priestess had cast and felt her consciousness
slipping
away.

And yet...

The amulet around Essie's neck began to glow, and she suddenly found
herself restored to perfect health as the medallion crumbled to dust.

Employing her great speed to her advantage, she immediately placed
another
amulet of life-saving around her neck (somewhere along the way, she
had
found a second spare in addition to Vlad's) and turned the priestess
into a
statue before she could react.

Unfortunately, directly behind the priestess was Pestilence. She
attempted
to use a wand of teleportation to remove him from her path, but it was
empty;
fortunately, she had another wand of teleportation which she put to
use.

Unfortunately, this provided Pestilence with ample time to live up to
its
name.

Unable to react quickly enough to deal with the fearsome rider, Essie
felt
disease consume her body.

Ultimately, her battle with Pestilence consumed both of the spare
amulets
of life-saving before she was able to break away from his clutches,
but she
survived!

It was not long afterwards that Essie arrived, at long last, at the
high
altar of Loki, and presented the Amulet of Yendor to Loki.

An invisible choir sang, and Essie was bathed in radiance...

Loki said, "Congratulations, mortal! In return for thy service, I
grant
thee the gift of Immortality!"

Essie felt uplifted, as though she was herself, becoming a
demigoddess--

----

Essie awoke with a start, very much alive and healthy in the physical
sense, though the thought of becoming a demigoddess certainly couldn't
hurt
one's spirits.

It was the latest in a series of recurring nightmares which had
occurred
for the past several nights, though, in opposition to most of the rest
of
them, it had ended on a high note, and not, as so many of its
predecessors
had, with a vision of her own horrible death.

The door to Essie's room creaked open to reveal an acolyte standing at
the
threshold. He stared at her for a moment, frowning; she returned the
stare
coldly.

"Oh, you're awake already?" said the acolyte. "Another nightmare?"

Essie stared at him. "Not exactly," she said.


